README-CIO -- the starred programs, and the five modules that serve them=======================================================================Some of the names in DOC/INDEX carry a star.  A star means the program needsone of Microware's runtime trap modules: `cio` for nearly all of them, andfor some `math`, `math881`, `csl` or `csl020`.THEY RUN HERE.  All five modules are on this disk, in CMDS, included withMicroware's permission -- SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  You need donothing, supply nothing, and fetch nothing.  The star tells you what aprogram depends on, not what it lacks.This file explains what the star means, and what to do in the two caseswhere you might want a different arrangement.Why so many programs need it----------------------------`cc -qixm` -- the C library in a shared trap handler rather than linked intoevery binary -- was the normal way to build for OS-9, and it is why a starredprogram is often a fraction of the size of a trap-free one.  Much of thiscollection was rebuilt trap-free (`-qm`) so it would run with nothing loadedat all; most of the programs under CMDS need no module whatever.  The restwere left as their authors built them.A program that cannot find its trap handler stops at once with    **** Can't install trap handler ****That is the message to recognise.  It is not a broken program, and on thisdisk you should never see it -- if you do, the five modules have been removedor the execution directory is not this disk's CMDS.The list was MEASURED, by running every program on a disk with the fivemodules taken away and matching that message.  It was never derived fromlooking for the string `cio` inside a binary: that proxy is wrong in bothdirections, and this collection has the scars to prove it -- `vi_nocio`contains the string and does not need the module, while `cyberwar`, `gnuchess`and `g` lack it and do.If you would rather use your own--------------------------------You run OS-9, so you have these modules already, and you may prefer your copy-- a different edition, or one matched to your processor.  Two ways:1. KEEP YOUR OWN /dd.  Point os9exec at your system disk and mount this one   alongside, so /dd stays yours and the collection is simply another drive:       OS9DISK=/path/to/your/os9disk  OS9H1=/path/to/freeware/dd  os9exec shell       chx /h1/CMDS   Your `cio` is found first, from your own /dd/CMDS.2. REPLACE THE COPIES HERE.  Overwrite them in this disk's CMDS:       copy /h1/CMDS/cio  /dd/CMDS/cio       copy /h1/CMDS/math /dd/CMDS/math   The disk you end up with then contains modules from your own SDK, so keep   that copy for your own use; the collection as published stays as it is.3. LOAD IT, wherever it is.  A trap handler has to be reachable through   the EXECUTION directory, which is why the message appears at all when   you run from somewhere else.  Putting the module in the module   directory first settles it wherever you are:       load /dd/CMDS/cio   CMDS/load is on this disk (source in SRC/load), so this needs nothing   fetched.  It is also how the six RTF Fortran programs get `os9lib' --   DOC/README-FORTRAN.   MEASURED 2026-08-28, both ways round: with the execution directory   moved to a directory holding no cio, `banner HI' stops with the   message above; after `load /dd/CMDS/cio' in the same session it   draws its banner.`math` is needed only by the programs that do floating point -- seeDOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT, which also explains when you want `math881`instead.DOC/README-RUNNING covers where the collection should be mounted, and thehandful of programs that look for files under /h0.If you take the modules away----------------------------Someone redistributing a subset, or testing what is genuinely free-standing,may want the disk without them.  Remove all five from CMDS and most of theprograms still run -- that is the whole point of the rebuild work.  Thestarred ones stop with the message above until a licensed `cio` is put back.Where a starred program mattered, there is usually a trap-free alternativealready here:    starred           use instead    -------           -----------    *ed  *emacs       vi, sedt, sed    *vi_cio           vi (the EFFO build)    *ksh              bash, sh, gshell    *zoo              lha, tar, gzip    *fgrep            grep    *ckermit          kermit    *cat.cio          cat    *rcs and friends  no free replacement on this diskDOC/INDEX marks every one of them, so you can tell before you try.A FEW cio PROGRAMS FAIL EVEN WITH cio PRESENT -- and here is how to tell------------------------------------------------------------------------Two programs on this disk answer with hundreds of lines of    No more memory !!!and do their job not at all:    echo 255 | cvtbase d h    setenv PORT /term ; logisim /dd/DOC/logisim/flipflop.lsiThat message comes from the EMULATOR, not from the program, which is why itreads like a broken machine.  It is neither.  Root cause found 2026-08-31:  A cio program reaches the C library through TRAP #13 with a selector.  The  archives here were linked against a `cio.l' whose table has `_flshbuf' at  selector $41.  Every `cio' MODULE anyone has -- ours, the SDK's -- puts a  MEMORY routine there instead.  So the program's `putc' calls what it thinks  is "flush this FILE" and reaches "allocate this many bytes", handing over  the FILE's ADDRESS as the byte count.  The allocation succeeds, the FILE is  never updated, and the next character does it again.  A few hundred  thousand times.  The cio module that would match those binaries is on none of these disks.WHAT THIS MEANS WHEN YOU MEET IT.  The program is not repairable from hereand no amount of memory helps.  Use the alternative in the table above.WHICH PROGRAMS.  353 modules here link cio; 41 contain the call that can dothis; 2 are confirmed to reach it.  The difference matters: the call onlyfires if the program runs a `putc' or `getc' MACRO on a FILE.  Anything thatprints through `printf', `fprintf' or `fwrite' is unaffected -- which is whyevery one of the 41 prints its own usage text perfectly, and why 368 starredprograms each passed 272,000 bytes of input through with no trouble at all.If you meet it in a program not named here, that is the shape to recognise:usage text fine, real work impossible, and the emulator's voice in place ofthe program's.